                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "東周列萌志 Philosophic Love is a free-to-play otome visual novel set during China's Warring States period, more than 2,300 years ago. I play as a modern woman who unexpectedly travels back in time and becomes involved with six men representing different philosophical schools, including Confucianism, Mohism, Daoism, Legalism and the School of Yin-Yang. The game has more than 500,000 words of script, so this is much more focused on story, relationships and choices than traditional gameplay.\n\nThe historical setting is easily the most interesting part for me. Instead of using the Warring States period simply as a background for a generic romance, the game actually builds its characters around the philosophical ideas of the period. Conversations and debates can therefore feel like more than standard dating-sim dialogue, because I am also being introduced to different schools of thought and the way their beliefs clash.\n\nThe relationship system is where the game gets most of its replay value. My choices can affect relationships and ultimately determine which character's route I follow, with six different men offering very different personalities and perspectives. I like visual novels that give me reasons to replay them rather than treating the first playthrough as the entire experience, and the multiple routes make that work particularly well. Steam also lists 47 achievements, giving completion-focused players another reason to explore different outcomes.\n\nThe biggest drawback is the language situation. The game supports Traditional Chinese and Simplified Chinese for its interface, audio and subtitles, but **English is not supported**. That is a major limitation for me because the entire experience depends on understanding a very large amount of written dialogue. Even though the game has received a very positive response from its existing audience, the lack of English makes it inaccessible to a large number of potential players.\n\nThe presentation has a distinctly anime-inspired visual-novel style, which suits the character-focused story well. It is not trying to compete with a modern big-budget RPG, and I think that is fine because the writing, character routes and historical setting are clearly the priorities. The free-to-play base game is also generous in terms of content, although there are paid extras including an update pack, soundtrack, additional save slots and an art book.\n\nWhat ultimately makes Philosophic Love interesting is how specifically it commits to its concept. It is not just an otome game with historical characters pasted onto it; the six romantic interests are connected to actual schools of Chinese philosophy, and the setting gives the story plenty of opportunities for debates, cultural details and political events. That makes it much more distinctive than a typical romance visual novel.\n\nOverall, 東周列萌志 Philosophic Love is a surprisingly ambitious historical otome visual novel with a strong setting, six distinct romance routes and an enormous amount of written content. I really like the combination of romance and Chinese philosophy, while the lack of English support is a serious barrier and the experience will obviously appeal much more to players who already enjoy visual novels. For its intended audience, though, it is an unusually interesting free-to-play visual novel with plenty of story to explore.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
